#{[17592186100640 :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{not extr.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) >       0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) <    0.$ So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)"] [17592186100641 :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <               0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2, \\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\n- We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2),$ the point at $x=2$ must be lower than the point at $x=1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3a15ad7af8061bb800c5536fe054594402c7898ef7485a8a4165aff9cdc272d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(2,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 3$, we must have $f''(x) >   0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,3).$ Also, since $f'(x)$ is constant and positive to the right of $x = 3$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing  straight line on $(3,\\infty).$\n\n![](images/20/200f45be73f0c38d5cab4099ac83ab5d80022539e17cd0996f1ab0228a90a89e.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/16/16aff3b702623f3b6339cacfcdac4c9b43900e58f1228481d168b8b0535e1412.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"] [17592186100642 :question/worked-solution "First, we summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undef.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f''$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undef.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\searrow\\:\\:\\frown$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=0$, where the derivative is undefined.\n- Since $x=0$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n- The graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](images/d6/d6e0cc01ff01e3dbacf6c0c92e2fdb1c88bd7b634f6eeb1f72102c8bc3e4ac7d.png)"] [17592186100643 :question/worked-solution "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f''$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\textrm{not extr.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow\\:\\:\\smile$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n- The graph must go down to the left of $x=1$ and go up to the right of that point.\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty,1)$ and concave up on $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a6271a56fc9a7e6cd81e87fa1e3f7a0142934dc129615372ab8c543400b57cfb.png)"] [17592186100652 :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/00/0082f0e8263af8c4c17f514f577efc7605fa7178a6fc3842cefd48d4f4cd3d74.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. Since the hypotenuse of the triangle has length $2,$ then by the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} & =(2)^{2}-{l}^{2}\\,⟹\\,b=\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l) = 2 + l + \\sqrt{4-l^2}.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $2,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0< l <2.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{4-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =4-{l}^{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =2 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l = \\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Interval</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, \\sqrt2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sqrt2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$( \\sqrt2,2)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, $l = \\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\sqrt{2}) & =2+\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{4-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2+\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{4-2} \\\\  & =2+\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100657 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a diagram of the situation:\n\n![](images/54/5418d6b54ee048f36737d0a62cc8a8c1a1086e10c3d52595d29e2cf88a0aab74.png)\n\nLet $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y= 4 - \\dfrac{4}{3} x.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA = xy = x \\left(  4 - \\dfrac{4}{3} x \\right) = 4x - \\dfrac{4}{3} x^2.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 4 - \\dfrac{8}{3} x,\n$$\n\nand solving $A'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4-\\frac{8}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}x & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Interval</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\left(0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2},3\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =4\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\frac{4}{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{4}{3}\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100662 :question/problem "The values of functions $h(x)$ and $z(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-1$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$h(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$h'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{h(x)}{z(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=-1.$"] [17592186100749 :question/problem "The values of functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-3$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$q'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\big( p(x) + 5q(x) \\big)$ at $x=-3.$"] [17592186100750 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\big(f(x)g(x)\\big)$ at $x=0.$"] [17592186100762 :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) <             0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ So, $f''(x) >  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/1567dcdd3dac0eb6b76b71e20466a77457a3d956c85be96048e5d3a94568c03a.png)"] [17592186100763 :question/worked-solution "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get $f'(x) <         0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{undef.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x \\in (-\\infty,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is an increasing straight line. So, $y=f'(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\n- For $x \\in (-1,\\infty),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, $f''(x) <  0$ and $y=f'(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y=f'(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y=f'(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d00aadf7b1666ea8a762833d1844d638a224759c58d1b06929657ddb90dc4abb.png)"] [17592186100766 :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n\n$$\nx=-2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <          0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-2.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty),$ the point at $x=-2$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b599481e7a73ec38b6f0f627b1b9a551c65e6cd3875b3789f0e7ebfa03eb786.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-\\infty,-2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/1a/1ae0017fd07d6e9fa04f233ca31ab89a07260a5dbc5557e4c8ae35ff91d61f43.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c215907aa0cc156e92ab3b6710d844e774df3149656fc2f71f27ee01db87382e.png)"] [17592186100767 :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/57/57937f50c8adac57cd0a8b131ae2605466fe5e87e85ba324977a95b2f2c7a006.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <      0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{no extr.}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/29/296d0c0e63bedc37841aa914e0fb07320fff634ea1f6ac019a84cc47b87966b4.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and on $(-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down the left of $x=-1$ and goes up to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) <      0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) >    0.$ So, $x =-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4fb55a07e1b55448ec2a2ada4fea07f363542cdf04a8676c90938b5c4f7fbe.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/df/dfeb793809943dc496cc1f47b26f9b20aa3403b6d999787a7444ede605547226.png)"] [17592186100773 :question/worked-solution "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis gives us the following information about the graph of $f$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x)$ must pass through the point $(2,3)$, and\n- $f(x)$ is increasing on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each graph:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Passes through $(2,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{green}\\checkmark$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{green}\\checkmark$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{green}\\checkmark$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{\\times}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c68ff82f142b8f7f3a41738ab3f39dcd6b55184aa277fe66bdcaf8d6e6c504f.png)"] [17592186100782 :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/1c/1ce7e5168c729917318b00b8ffaafaef699b9a0e447ee3493a3d76f51d9d5d92.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{72-l^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l) = \\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{72-l^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<           l<           6\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $A'(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}}) & =2\\sqrt{72-{l}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\ 72-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =36 \\\\ l & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Interval</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0,6)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(6, 6\\sqrt{2})$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$P$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, $l=6$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(6) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\sqrt{72-{(6)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{72-36} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186101942 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we only know the values of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ at $x=0$ along with their derivatives, as given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCan we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big )$ at $x=0?$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) = f'(x)g(x)+f(x)g'(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Big( f(x)g(x)\\Big) \\right|_{x=0}\t=\tf'(0)g(0)+f(0)g'(0).\n$$\n\nAll of these values are given in the table! So, substituting the values from the table into the formula, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & ={f}^{′}(0)g(0)+f(0){g}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186101990 :tutorial/content "The following table shows the first six binomial expansions.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Power</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Pascal's Triangle</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Binomial</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Binomial Expansion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}a + \\mathbf{1}b$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{2}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}a^2 + \\mathbf{2}ab + \\mathbf{1}b^2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}a^3 + \\mathbf{3}a^2b + \\mathbf{3}ab^2 + \\mathbf{1}b^3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{6}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\!\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(a + b)^5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf{1}a^5 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}a^4b \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^3b^2 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^2b^3 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}ab^4 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{1}b^5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186101997 :tutorial/content "The function $f(x)$ is differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2$. The values of $f$ and $f'$ are given in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's find\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we try to substitute $x=2$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= \\left.(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8)\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f(2))/(2^{3} - 8) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= lim_(x → 2)((f(x))^{′})/((x^{3} - 8)^{′}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2$ again, and this time, we are able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2}) &= \\left.(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f^{′}(2))/(3 \\cdot 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3 \\cdot 2^{2} - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"] [17592186102004 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x),$ as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and an inflection point at $x=0.5.$\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)?$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-1,2)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(2,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5.$ So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)"] [17592186102005 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Let's use this to sketch a possible graph of the original function, $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd6c0fa999d571b54b7408856ecc8d091c2f4bb16b9f7bf85eb7f91b05629e4.png)\n\nRecall that the critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-3\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,-3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-3,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(1,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0de4712344b43d32343f4d9fbc11d3dbc5c96df858f27bee599a3ffdd448ce.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-3)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/34/3498651b6a642f59068f23e8ed9ddd211bfb76ab4d0bf8e28cefd84d1a4b2aed.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/257203aa25e423bf82ce745a6a8a3563aa80e414e9dfadf0be64629624cbce0e.png)"] [17592186102007 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to sketch a possible graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f'(3)=0$\n- if $x \\in (-\\infty,3)$ then $f'(x)> 0$\n- if $x \\in (3,+\\infty)$ then $f'(x)<0$\n- $f''(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n\nWe summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(-\\infty,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(3,\\infty)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f''$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\textrm{max}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\searrow\\:\\:\\frown$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=3$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n- Since $x=3$ is a local maximum, the graph must go down to the left and to the right of that point.\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down everywhere.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7c6d8412e9c657e8e4a148e40ca0b8a073735d3b88681eec6a79f763471aaf4f.png)"] [17592186105446 :question/problem "The values of the function $f(x)$ and its derivative at $x=-1$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind $g'\\left(-1\\right)$, if $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{f(x)}$."] [17592186105447 :question/problem "The table below shows the values of functions $f$, $g$, $f'$, and $g'$ at $x=1$ and $x=2$. Find $h'(2)$, if $h(x)=g(f(x))$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105449 :question/problem "The values of the function $f(x)$ and its derivative at $x = -3$ are given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e^{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind $w'(-3)$, if $w(x) = e^{f(x)}$."] [17592186105495 :question/problem "![](images/78/78e255adadf62e73ccf11f3f7b597a4c8d3abb31f0c466a6cfd73d289c4c1799.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are consecutive interior angles</td>\n<td>Definition of consecutive interior angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105495 :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af459fd393070011074661807dd094d98ecb32f256816c5fa517b11e7d696c1f.png)\n\nThe correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a parallelogram}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are consecutive interior angles</td>\n<td>Definition of consecutive interior angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Consecutive interior angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. By the definition of a parallelogram, its opposite sides are parallel. This means that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$.\n- Consider row 4. From row 3, we know that $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are consecutive interior angles. Therefore, by the consecutive interior angles theorem,\n$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary.\n- Consider row 5. From row 4, we have that $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary angles. Therefore, by the definition of supplementary angles, we have\n$m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$."] [17592186105496 :question/problem "In the diagram below, $L$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HJ}$ and $\\overline{IK}$.\n\n![](images/ae/aee6d4eb9106540cfde13d76cbfb57803cb8040832f5eef11aacc6145f10747d.png)\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *$HIJK$ is a parallelogram.*\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$L$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HJ}$ and $\\overline{IK}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{HL} \\cong \\overline{JL}$ and $\\overline{IL} \\cong \\overline{KL}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ILJ} \\cong \\angle{KLH}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ILJ} \\cong \\triangle{KLH}$</td>\n<td>SAS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{LIJ} \\cong \\angle{LKH}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{IJ} \\parallel \\overline{HK}$</td>\n<td>Converse alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle{HLI} \\cong \\angle{JLK}$</td>\n<td>Vertical angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{HLI} \\cong \\triangle{JLK}$</td>\n<td>SAS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle{HIL} \\cong \\angle{JKL}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Converse alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$HIJK$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Definition of a parallelogram</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105496 :question/worked-solution "![](images/b4/b4040de5dcfddd601310745462a86e2495dacb2474cd28b2ad9782e832f50f2b.png)\n\nThe correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$L$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HJ}$ and $\\overline{IK}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{HL} \\cong \\overline{JL}$ and $\\overline{IL} \\cong \\overline{KL}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a midpoint}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ILJ} \\cong \\angle{KLH}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Vertical angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ILJ} \\cong \\triangle{KLH}$</td>\n<td>SAS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{LIJ} \\cong \\angle{LKH}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{IJ} \\parallel \\overline{HK}$</td>\n<td>Converse alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle{HLI} \\cong \\angle{JLK}$</td>\n<td>Vertical angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{HLI} \\cong \\triangle{JLK}$</td>\n<td>SAS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle{HIL} \\cong \\angle{JKL}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}{\\overline{HI} \\parallel \\overline{JK}}}$</td>\n<td>Converse alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$HIJK$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Definition of a parallelogram</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, $L$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HJ}$ and $\\overline{IK}$. Therefore, by the definition of a midpoint, $\\overline{HL} \\cong \\overline{JL}$ and $\\overline{IL} \\cong \\overline{KL}$.\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle{ILJ}$ and $\\angle{KLH}$ are vertical angles. Therefore, by the vertical angles theorem, $\\angle{ILJ}\\cong\\angle{KLH}$.\n- Consider row 10. Notice that the transversal $\\overline{IK}$ crosses the lines $\\overline{HI}$ and $\\overline{JK}$, forming the alternate interior angles $\\angle{HIL}$ and $\\angle{JKL}$. From row 9, we know that $\\angle{HIL} \\cong \\angle{JKL}$. Therefore, by the converse alternate interior angles theorem,\n$\\overline{HI} \\parallel \\overline{JK}$."] [17592186105497 :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cfcadfb9587ae045faef7e406b257770566f526b759c9127fc431bc4dc50bec.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{C}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Fill in the blanks to complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ABD} \\cong \\angle{CDB}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ADB} \\cong \\angle{CBD}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{BD}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{CDB}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{C}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{C}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105497 :question/worked-solution "![](images/32/324babc470c99fc8ca507b6e31c82cec634c598ad37847d03803c72a58ac97cb.png)\n\nThe correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a parallelogram}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ABD} \\cong \\angle{CDB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a parallelogram}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{ADB} \\cong \\angle{CBD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Alternate interior angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{BD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{CDB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ASA congruence criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{C}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{C}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We are given that $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.\n2. By the definition of a parallelogram, its opposite sides are parallel. Therefore, $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$.\n3. From row 2, $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{CD}$. Note that the transversal $\\overline{BD}$ crosses the parallel lines $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$, forming the alternate interior angles $\\angle{ABD}$ and $\\angle{CDB}$. Therefore, by the alternate interior angles theorem,\n$\\angle{ABD} \\cong \\angle{CDB}$.\n4. By the definition of a parallelogram, its opposite sides are parallel. Therefore, $\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$.\n5. From row 4, $\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$. Note that the transversal $\\overline{BD}$ crosses parallel lines $\\overline{AD}$ and $\\overline{BC}$, forming the alternate interior angles $\\angle{ADB}$ and $\\angle{CBD}$. Therefore, by the alternate interior angles theorem,\n$\\angle{ADB} \\cong \\angle{CBD}$.\n6. By the reflexive property of congruence, every segment is congruent to itself. Therefore, $\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{BD}$.\n7. Notice that $\\triangle{ABD}$ and $\\triangle{CDB}$ have a common side $\\overline{BD}$ and two pairs of congruent angles adjacent to it, namely, $\\angle{ABD} \\cong \\angle{CDB}$ and $\\angle{ADB} \\cong \\angle{CBD}$. Therefore, by the ASA criterion,\n$\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{CDB}$.\n8. From row 7, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}A}{\\color{blue}B}D} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}C}{\\color{blue}D}B}$. Therefore, we get $\\angle{{\\color{red}A}} \\cong \\angle{{\\color{red}C}}$ by CPCTC.\n9. From row 8, $\\angle{A} \\cong \\angle{C}$. Therefore, $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{C}$ by the definition of congruent angles."] [17592186105499 :question/problem "Consider the following disconnected graph.\n\n![](images/92/92d32d3ff97b9602be4c2ae10a778be395e749ae91d5be819a0e4953b8f96de6.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105499 :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in an undirected graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nIf there is no path form $v_i$ and $v_j$, we define\n\n$$\nd_{ij}=\\infty\n$$\n\nThe distances are calculated as follows:\n\n- First, the entry $d_{11}$ is the distance from $v_1$ to itself, which is always zero. Hence,\n$d_{11} = 0$.\n- Now, recall that the distance matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric about the main diagonal. Hence, by symmetry, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle d_{15} = d_{51} = 2.\n$$\n- Finally, the distance from $v_2$ to $v_4$ is $\\infty$ because there is no path from $v_2$ to $v_4$. Hence, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle d_{24} = d_{42} = \\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105500 :question/problem "Consider the following graph.\n\n![](images/d0/d0dca731ce03691b5bf868071465b7ccb500f9c9e3fe824b43c9c1251c832bdf.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105500 :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance from the vertex $v_i$ to the vertex $v_j$.\n\nIn our case, the vertices are arranged as $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4$, and $v_5$. Let's determine the distances.\n\nRecall that the distance matrix of a directed graph is not symmetric in general. Some of the distances are calculated as follows:\n\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_2$ is $1$ since the shortest path from $v_1$ to $v_2$ (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2$) has a length of $1$. Hence, we have\n$d_{12} = 1$.\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_3$ is $2$ since the shortest path from $v_1$ to $v_3$ (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$) has a length of $2$. Hence, we have\n$d_{13} = 2$.\n\n- The entry $d_{33}$ is the distance from $v_3$ to itself, which is always $0$. Hence,\n$d_{33} = 0$.\n- The distance from $v_5$ to $v_2$ is $2$ since the shortest path from $v_5$ to $v_2$ (namely, $v_5 \\to v_1 \\to v_2$) has a length of $2$. Hence,\n$d_{52} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105501 :question/problem "Consider the following directed weighted graph.\n\n![](images/53/533d34061a801dab8ddc970cdd2f90ab6f7335678b03af29a5d53bb63bffaaa6.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105501 :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed weighted graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nIf there is no path from $v_i$ to $v_j$, we define\n\n$$\nd_{ij}=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn our case, the vertices are arranged as $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4$, and $v_5$. Some of the distance calculations are here as follows:\n\n- The entry $d_{11}$ is the distance from $v_1$ to itself, which is always zero. Hence,\n$d_{11} = 0$.\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_3$ is $7$ because the shortest path between these vertices (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$, not $v_1 \\to v_3$) has a total length of $3+4=7$. Hence, we have\n$d_{13} = 7$.\n- The distance from $v_2$ to $v_5$ is $8$ because the shortest path between these vertices (namely, $v_2 \\to v_4 \\to v_5$) has a total length of $3+5=8$. Hence, we have\n$d_{25} = 8$.\n- The distance from $v_3$ to $v_2$ is $\\infty$ because there is no path from $v_3$ to $v_3$. Hence, we have\n$d_{32} = \\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105503 :question/problem "In the diagram below, $\\overline{TU}$ bisects both $\\angle{VUW}$ and $\\angle{VTW}$.\n\n![](images/dd/dd969699d09cd8d53298f43d33ee980459edb5d765378e08bd4633928fc7aa2e.png)\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\overline{TW} \\cong \\overline{TV}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Fill in the blanks to complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU}$ bisects $\\angle{VUW}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{TUV} \\cong \\angle{TUW}$</td>\n<td>Definition of an angle bisector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU}$ bisects $\\angle{VTW}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{UTV} \\cong \\angle{UTW}$</td>\n<td>Definition of an angle bisector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU} \\cong \\overline{TU}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{TUV} \\cong \\triangle{TUW}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TW} \\cong \\overline{TV}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105503 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU}$ bisects $\\angle{VUW}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle{TUV} \\cong \\angle{TUW}$</td>\n<td>Definition of an angle bisector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU}$ bisects $\\angle{VTW}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{UTV} \\cong \\angle{UTW}$</td>\n<td>Definition of an angle bisector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TU} \\cong \\overline{TU}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{TUV} \\cong \\triangle{TUW}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ASA congruence criterion}}$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{TW} \\cong \\overline{TV}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 5. We have that $\\overline{TU}$ is congruent to itself by the reflexive property of congruence.\n- Consider row 6. From rows 2 and 4, we know that $\\angle{TUV} \\cong \\angle{TUW}$ and $\\angle{UTV} \\cong \\angle{UTW}$. Also, $\\overline{TU}$ is a common side of $\\triangle{TUV}$ and $\\triangle{TUW}$. Therefore, $\\triangle{TUV} \\cong \\triangle{TUW}$ by the ASA congruence criterion.\n- Consider row 7. From row 6, $\\triangle{TUV} \\cong \\triangle{TUW}$. Therefore, $\\overline{TW} \\cong \\overline{TV}$ by CPCTC."] [17592186105504 :question/problem "![](images/10/10bc31ebbdcaf86554eaa9bcef96f39c1e45b4088e03307cf7d1043f414a158c.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m\\angle{Q} = m\\angle{R}$ and $QT = RS$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\triangle{PQT} \\cong \\triangle{PRS}$\n\nThe table below provides proof of the statement. Complete the proof by filling in the empty boxes.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{P} \\cong \\angle{P}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{Q} = m\\angle{R}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{Q} \\cong \\angle{R}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$QT = RS$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{QT} \\cong \\overline{RS}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{PQT} \\cong \\triangle{PRS}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105504 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle{P} \\cong \\angle{P}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle{Q} = m\\angle{R}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{Q} \\cong \\angle{R}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$QT = RS$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{QT} \\cong \\overline{RS}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent segments}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{PQT} \\cong \\triangle{PRS}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{AAS congruence criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We have that $\\angle{P}$ is congruent to itself by the reflexive property of congruence.\n2. We're given that $m\\angle{Q} = m\\angle{R}$.\n3. From row 2, $m\\angle{Q} = m\\angle{R}$. Therefore, $\\angle{Q} \\cong \\angle{R}$ by the definition of congruent angles.\n4. We're given that $QT = RS$.\n5. From row 4, $QT = RS$. Therefore, $\\overline{QT} \\cong \\overline{RS}$ by the definition of congruent segments.\n6. From rows 1 and 3, $\\angle{P} \\cong \\angle{P}$ and $\\angle{Q} \\cong \\angle{R}$. Also, from row 5, $\\overline{QT} \\cong \\overline{RS}$. Therefore, $\\triangle{PQT} \\cong \\triangle{PRS}$ by the AAS congruence criterion."] [17592186105505 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c41e3624b569623450497bd79cdcd67818dcf85884ddff22277a947a1ec7c3ab.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $O$ is the midpoint of the segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{NK}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $\\overline{NM} \\cong \\overline{KL}$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Fill in the missing reasons and thus complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$NO = OK$</td>\n<td>Definition of a midpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{NO} \\cong \\overline{OK}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$MO = OL$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{MO} \\cong \\overline{OL}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{NOM} \\cong \\angle{KOL}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{NOM} \\cong \\triangle{KOL}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{NM} \\cong \\overline{KL}$</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105505 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c4/c41e3624b569623450497bd79cdcd67818dcf85884ddff22277a947a1ec7c3ab.png)\n\nThe correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$NO = OK$</td>\n<td>Definition of a midpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{NO} \\cong \\overline{OK}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$MO = OL$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a midpoint}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{MO} \\cong \\overline{OL}$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\angle{NOM} \\cong \\angle{KOL}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Vertical angles theorem}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{NOM} \\cong \\triangle{KOL}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS congruence criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{NM} \\cong \\overline{KL}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. We're given that $O$ is a midpoint of the segment $\\overline{LM}$. Therefore, by the definition of a midpoint, we have that $MO = OL$.\n- Consider row 5. Notice that the angles $\\angle{NOM}$ and $\\angle{KOL}$ are vertical angles. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the vertical angles theorem.\n- Consider row 6. From rows 2 and 4, we know that $\\overline{NO} \\cong \\overline{OK}$ and $\\overline{MO} \\cong \\overline{OL}$. Also, from row 5, we know that the angles between these sides are congruent. Therefore, $\\triangle{NOM} \\cong \\triangle{KOL}$ by the SAS congruence criterion.\n- Consider row 7. From row 6, we have that $\\triangle{NOM} \\cong \\triangle{KOL}$. Therefore, by CPCTC, we can conclude that $\\overline{NM} \\cong \\overline{LK}$."] [17592186105567 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-\\sqrt{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\sqrt{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">min</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">max</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$"] [17592186105569 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."] [17592186105570 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-\\dfrac{5}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">min</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"] [17592186105577 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105578 :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$"] [17592186105578 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx\tf(2)\\Delta x_1+f(3)\\Delta x_2+f(3.5)\\Delta x_3+f(4)\\Delta x_4,\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i}-x_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1+8.5\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4+8.5+5+4 \\\\  & =21.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105648 :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-4,2].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $12.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105648 :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula for the right Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+k+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k+10 & =12\\\\[5pt]\n      2k &= 12-10\\\\[5pt]\n        k &= \\dfrac{2}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n         k  &= 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105649 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."] [17592186105653 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X=x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{2}{5}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P\\left( X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above."] [17592186105654 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above."] [17592186105657 :question/problem "Let $X$ be the number obtained by a spinner with four sections labeled $1, 2, 3, 4.$ The probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown in the table below. What is $\\textrm{Var}[X]?$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186105658 :question/worked-solution "The standard deviation of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{Var}[X] },\n$$\n\nwhere the variance can be computed using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x).$ Here, there are $6$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $\\textrm E[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X^2$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+9\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+25\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+36\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{35}{12}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105662 :question/worked-solution "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E[X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x).$ Here, there are $6$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we compute $\\textrm E[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X^2$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+9\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+25\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+36\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{6}-\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105673 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor the function, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6} = \\dfrac{1}{(x-3)(x+2)}\n$$\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ has two vertical asymptotes, $x=-2$ and $x=3$.\n\nSince $x=3$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x \\to 3^+$ will be either $+\\infty$ or $-\\infty$.\n\nTo find the limit as $x \\to 3^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=3$ from the right:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.001$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx 1.96$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx 19.96$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx 199.96$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd067e14ed7eb16ef1fd8a6212e02c88c79f855bfe1b23290c45538766a15ca9.png)"] [17592186105674 :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2} + 2x - x - 2} \\\\\n&= (1)/(x(x + 2) - (x + 2)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((x - 1)(x + 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the function $f(x)$ has two vertical asymptotes, $x=-2$ and $x=1$.\n\nSince $x=1$ is a vertical asymptote, the limit as $x \\to 1^-$ will be either $+\\infty$ or $-\\infty$.\n\nTo find the limit is $x \\to 1^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the left:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.999$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx -3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx -33$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\approx -333$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^-} f(x)= -\\infty\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48d76ae5415252614009a94dfdaded6d67dc64c066ca8d07333c53680b5f93aa.png)"] [17592186105675 :question/worked-solution "The function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nhas a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x \\to 1$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=1$ from the right and from the left.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9\\,900$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{undefined}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$110$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAll the values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger as $x\\to1$ from both sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^-} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)= +\\infty\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is false and II is true.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=1$ are equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x)= +\\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05fab8ba6b5fc4497395750653563907d8c3a5cefe4941918cfebf7f807dedf9.png)"] [17592186105678 :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2 - x}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= - ((x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\neq \\pm 2\n$$\n\n, and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\nf(x) =-\\frac{1}{x + 2}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has only one vertical asymptote, $x=-2$. So statements II and III are false.\n\nTo find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to (-2)^+}f(x),\n$$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-2$ from the right.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1.999$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1\\,000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAll the values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative as $x\\to-2$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to (-2)^+}f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore statement I is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f49f0fab0cc9b8182a15167f6777de2062ae4feed4d8049a59c260e7206f354a.png)"] [17592186105688 :question/problem "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below.  Find $\\textrm{E}\\big[5e^{1-X}\\big].$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186106029 :question/problem "A discrete random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. Find $E[(X - 1)^{2}]$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106233 :question/problem "![](images/42/42c5eefced4b7e41c09466922362a5b5dd66fd5621caeeb5c206a4c6e829bf41.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $△ACD$ is equilateral, $AB^(―)≅AE^(―)$, and $∠BAC≅∠DAE$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $△BAC≅△EAD$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$△ACD$ is equilateral</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>{{field-1}}</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$∠BAC≅∠DAE$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>{{field-2}}</td>\n<td>{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$△BAC≅△EAD$</td>\n<td>{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186106364 :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[- 2, 7]$. The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y = f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $30$. What must be the value of $k$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106367 :question/problem "Pauline is selling orange juice on a market stall. The table below gives the rate $l(t)$, in liters per hour, of lemonade sold at certain moments during the day.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t(hours)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$l(t)(liters/hour)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $l(t)$ over the interval $[1, 7]$ with three subintervals to approximate the total number of liters Pauline sold.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106368 :question/problem "The table below gives the rate of change $w(t)$, in pounds per year, in a child's weight when the child is exactly $t$ years old.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t(years)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$w(pounds/year)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[1, 4]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total increase in the weight of the child.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106486 :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[2, 8]$. The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y = f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $14$. What must be the value of $k$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106495 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P(2 \\le Y < 4)$ given that the random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106496 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P(Y ∈ {0, 1, 3, 4})$ given that the random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106500 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X=x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{4}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above."] [17592186106501 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{4}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{9}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nCompute $P(X \\ge 1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106502 :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $Var[X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106505 :question/problem "Several children were asked their age. The age of each child (in years) was recorded below. Based on this data, if $X$ is the age of a randomly selected child, what is the standard deviation of $X$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Age</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Number of Children</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106506 :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $Var[X]$?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106994 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted undirected graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple undirected unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix (table), where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric about the main diagonal.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/ef/efa72276c05169b41b045d91557a774569fe7ef6271d1a1dd9546cd339e98a41.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_2-v_3-v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{42}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Since the vertices $v_2$ and $v_3$ are adjacent, the shortest path between them is $1$. Hence, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{23}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{32}$. Similarly, since $v_3$ and $v_4$ are adjacent, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{34}$ and $a_{43}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Since the vertex $v_1$ is isolated, the distance from it to any other vertex is $\\infty$. Hence, we write $\\infty$ in every entry of the $1$st row and in every entry of the $1$st column, except for $a_{11}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}0 & \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [17592186107008 :tutorial/content "![](images/1a/1a5e76d241ef397c8a3f4ed2664723c63e5a20b654ca67a567d2efec5716a9b2.png)\n\nThe following table shows the proof that $\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$ in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{AD}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$</td>\n<td>SSS congruence criterion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186107010 :tutorial/content "![](images/04/0438c60066f3dcde0a6a5c70fc16b49dc5cea98eead984dfd95c2bddad4ff9fa.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof that opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$MNPQ$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{MN} \\parallel \\overline{PQ}, \\: \\overline{MQ} \\parallel \\overline{NP}$</td>\n<td>Definition of a parallelogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{MNQ} \\cong \\angle{NQP}, \\: \\angle{MQN} \\cong \\angle{QNP}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\overline{QN} \\cong \\overline{QN}$</td>\n<td>Reflexive property of congruence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{QMN} \\cong \\triangle{NPQ}$</td>\n<td>ASA criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\overline{MN} \\cong \\overline{PQ}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186107012 :tutorial/content "![](images/a3/a3a387f45449207a466c6e32e9b17c15d63295ee01947f5abaea402802e372fa.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other in a two-column format.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$VWXY$ is a parallelogram</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\overline{VW} \\parallel \\overline{XY}$</td>\n<td>Definition of a parallelogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle{VWZ} \\cong \\angle{XYZ}$</td>\n<td>Alternate interior angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle{VZW} \\cong \\angle{XZY}$</td>\n<td>Vertical angles theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$\\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$</td>\n<td>Property of a parallelogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{VWZ} \\cong \\triangle{XYZ}$</td>\n<td>AAS criterion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\overline{VZ} \\cong \\overline{XZ}$ and $\\overline{WZ} \\cong \\overline{YZ}$</td>\n<td>CPCTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$VZ=XZ$ and $WZ=YZ$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent segments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we'll consider a proof of the converse theorem:\n\n> *If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram.*"] [17592186107023 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted directed graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple directed unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric about the main diagonal in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e105ae2ce9cbdd8f4494b9a0356f136da06f60e93250c45348811b3bc8ce4cc.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has three edges and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $3$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_4 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_4$ and $v_3$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{43}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- We write $1$ in every entry that represents a vertex pair connected by a direct edge: $a_{12}, a_{23}, a_{34}$, and $a_{42}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}0 & 1 & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 3 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [17592186107024 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in a weighted graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., sum of the weights of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a weighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$. Here $G$ can be either directed or undirected.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected weighted graph is symmetric about the main diagonal, but the distance matrix of a directed weighted graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the directed weighted graph below.\n\n![](images/45/457cc5d27f10b40f82a6951b3d88964ca9455530adde7fcd8abb797f0f16fd32.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The detour path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $2+1+5=8$ and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. (Notice that the alternative path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_4$, which has fewer edges, has a greater weight of $2+7=9$.) Hence, we write $8$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $1+5=6$ and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $6$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- We write the weight of an edge in the entry corresponding to a vertex pair connected by a direct edge with no shorter detours exist: $a_{12}, a_{23}$, and $a_{34}$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v_4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\infty$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}0 & 2 & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 8 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0 & 5 \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & \\mathrm{\\infty } & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [17592186107031 :tutorial/content "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.99$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.999$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1\\,000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.01$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.001$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)"] [17592186107066 :tutorial/content "A stationary point of a function can be classified in one of three ways: as a local minimum, a local maximum, or neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTo classify a stationary point without using a graph, we can use the **first derivative test**. The first derivative states that, when crossing a stationary point from left to right:\n\n1\\. If the sign of the derivative switches from *negative to positive*, then the stationary point is a *local minimum* (because it lies at the bottom of a valley).\n\n2\\. If the sign of the derivative switches from *positive to negative*, then the stationary point is a *local maximum* (because it lies at the top of a peak).\n\n3\\. If the sign of the derivative *does not switch sign*, then the stationary point is *neither a local minimum nor a local maximum*.\n\nFor example, let's consider a function whose graph is depicted in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4c/4cd493709694d9d2db7c77c285877b4bd98c0e756c321f8920b0436a6f3ec08a.png)\n\nThe function has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=1$ and $x=3.$ Summarizing the information about the signs of the derivative from the picture, we obtain the following table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-1,1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(1,3\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(3, \\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, let's use the first derivative test to classify the local extrema:\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=3$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has \n\n- one local minimum at $x = -1,$\n\n- one local maximum at $x=1,$ and\n\n- neither a local minimum nor a local maximum at $x=3.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-1,1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(1,3\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(3, \\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">min</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">max</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">neither</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFFDE7\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186107068 :tutorial/content "The really useful thing about the first derivative test is that we can use it even if we don't have the graph of the function.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x) =  x^3 - 3x + 2.$ We can classify all the local extrema of this function without even graphing it.\n\nFirst, we need to find the stationary points. Taking the derivative, we have $f'(x)= 3x^2-3,$ and the stationary points of the function are located at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNow, let's classify each stationary point.\n\n- Let's start with $x=-1.$ We compute $f'(x)$ at two test points that are \"close\" to $x=-1,$ say $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2)^{2}-3=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-3=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize this information in the table below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-1,1\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Test Point</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">max</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo $f'(x)$ switches from positive to negative at $x=-1,$ and we conclude that $x=-1$ is a *local maximum*.\n\n- Now, let's test $x=1.$ We already know that $f'(x)$ is negative to the left of this point, so we just need to choose one more test point that is to the right of this point, say at $x=2.$ We get\n\n$$\nf'(2)= 3(2)^2-3 = 9> 0,\n$$\n\nand we include this information in our table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(-1,1\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">$\\left(1, \\infty\\right)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}+$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">max</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}\\searrow$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">min</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}\\nearrow$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo $f'(x)$ switches from negative to positive at $x=1,$ and we conclude that $x=1$ is a *local minimum*.\n\nIn conclusion, the stationary points are $x=-1,$ which is a local maximum, and $x=1,$ which is a local minimum."] [17592186107115 :tutorial/content "The **probability distribution**, or more precisely, **probability mass function** of a discrete random variable $X$ on a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that maps each element $x \\in S$ to its probability of occurring:\n\n$$\nP(X = x) = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe above can be read as \"the probability that the random variable $X$ takes on the value $x,$ is equal to $f(x).$\"\n\nFor example, if $X$ is the result of rolling a $6$-sided die, then we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} , \\hspace{1em} & x = 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis function tells us that\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any integer between $1$ and $6$ inclusive is $\\dfrac{1}{6},$ and\n\n- the probability of the die landing on any other number is zero.\n\nWe can also represent this probability mass function using a table, as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186107123 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f_X$ and support $S_X = \\{-1,0,1\\},$ shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow suppose that we define a new random variable $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nY = (X+1)^3.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ is quite a lot of work if we use only the methods we've seen up to now. Typically, we would need to find the probability mass function $f_Y$ of $Y,$ and then compute $\\textrm{E}[Y].$\n\nThankfully, there is a handy rule, called **the rule of the lazy statistician,** that allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ *without* having to find $f_Y$ first! This is useful because $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ might be the only quantity we're interested in. \n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician states that if $Y = r(X),$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] =  \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x).\n$$\n\nApplying the rule of the lazy statistician with the function $r(x) = (x+1)^3$ for our PMF $f_X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}r(x)\\,{f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}(x+1)^{3}\\,{f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =(-1+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.25+(0+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.5+(1+1)^{3}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & ={0}^{3}\\cdot 0.25+{1}^{3}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{3}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.25+1\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.5.$"] [17592186107143 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute derivatives of composite functions when given function data at specific points.\n\nFor example, suppose we know the values of two functions, $f(x), g(x)$, and their derivatives at the points $x=1$ and $x=2$. This data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g'(x)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{violet}-4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, let's define a new function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=f(g(x))\n$$\n\nBy the chain rule, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x)\n$$\n\nTo compute, for example, $h'(1)$, we substitute $x=1$ into the expression for $h'(x)$ above.\n\n$$\nh'(1) = f'(g(1)) \\cdot g'(1)\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate this derivative by substituting some values from the table above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(1) &= f^{′}(g(1)) \\cdot g^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= f^{′}(2) \\cdot g^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-4) \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh'(1) = -20\n$$"] [17592186107152 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t\\overbrace{\\left[\\: \\overbrace{\\color{red}f(1.5)}^{\\color{red}\\large\\textrm{height}} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x_1}_{\\color{blue}\\large\\textrm{width}} \\:\\right]}^{\\Large{ {1} \\textrm{st rectangle}}} \n\t\t\t\t+ \\overbrace{\\left[\\: \\overbrace{\\color{red}f(2.5)}^{\\color{red}\\large\\textrm{height}} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x_2}_{\\color{blue}\\large\\textrm{width}} \\:\\right]}^{\\Large{ {2} \\textrm{nd rectangle}}} \n\t\t\t\t+ \\overbrace{\\left[\\: \\overbrace{\\color{red}f(4)}^{\\color{red}\\large\\textrm{height}} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x_3}_{\\color{blue}\\large\\textrm{width}} \\:\\right]}^{\\Large{ {3} \\textrm{rd rectangle}}} \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\\\\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}  \\\\\n\t\t\t&=1+6+12\\\\\n\t\t\t&= 19.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of sides</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td>Triangle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td>Quadrilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td>Pentagon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td>Hexagon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td>Heptagon</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td>Octagon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td>Nonagon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td>Decagon</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186110337 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841,$ find $\\mu.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.7 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7580 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7611 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7642 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.8 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7881 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7910 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7939 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.9 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8159 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8186 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.8212 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186110338 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"